Five things stand out.
The improvement is not noise.
OLS cuts pooled mean squared error by 0.52%, with a Diebold-Mariano statistic of 25.82 and p below 0.0001 over 2,087 origins.
The gain is uneven.
OLS gains most on the finest levels, -0.75% and -0.89% at x1 and x2, between -0.20% and -0.77% in the middle, and turns into a small loss, +0.11%, at the daily node. Section 8 magnifies that last cell.
Textbook MinT is dangerous here.
The sample-covariance version inflated error by 108.77%; on a 250-residual window over the latest 30,000 bars by 830%, and on that window ending six weeks earlier by over 3,700%. The damage is as unstable as the estimate, which needed a ridge in 1,851 of 2,087 refits despite 750 residuals for sixty nodes: numerically singular most of the time, as section 4 predicted. Shrinkage repairs the long window, -0.50% at p below 0.0001 with intensity averaging 0.056, but on the short one reaches only -0.13% (p = 0.23) at intensity 0.149.
The two WLS columns are one column.
They never differ by as much as a tenth of a percent: the measured variances are proportional to the structural row sums, so the free estimate already knows what the measured one learns.
OLS was the safe choice here.
It has no covariance to get wrong and never blew up; MinT-shrink matched it, -0.50% against -0.52%, and beat it at x8 and x12. With the naive forecasts of section 8 both MinT variants beat OLS outright, -32.9% and -33.3% against -24.8%. Two hierarchies and three simple models cannot crown a general default; what survives both runs is that MinT buys its advantage with an estimate, and OLS cannot be charged for a bad one.
